Exercise 1.22 asks that the sets \([X]=\{x\in|\mathcal{D}|\mid X\in x\}\) be the basic neighbourhoods of a topology on \(|\mathcal{D}|\), that an open set be one which is upward closed under approximation and in which every point \(x\) admits some \(X\in x\) with \([X]\) contained in the set, and that \(x\sqsubseteq y\) if and only if every open set containing \(x\) contains \(y\); it then asks whether \(|\mathcal{D}|\) is ever Hausdorff, whether the union of an increasing sequence is a topological limit of that sequence, and whether \(x\) is a limit point of \(\{\uparrow X\mid X\in x\}\). `basicOpen` is \([X]\) and `OpenFilter` is the basic-neighbourhood condition, which `instTopologicalSpaceElement` in `Basic` takes as the definition of an open set: the whole space is the basic open of the master, the intersection of two opens uses the basic open of the intersection of the two witnessing neighbourhoods via `basicOpen_inter_subset_left` and `basicOpen_inter_subset_right`, and a union reuses the witness already present in one member. `isOpen_basicOpen` places each basic open among the opens; `isOpen_isUpperSet` deduces upward closure because any element above a point still contains the witnessing neighbourhood and therefore lies in its basic open; `isOpen_iff_upper_basic` is the conjunction of those two conditions, the reverse direction retaining only the basic-neighbourhood half because that half is openness; `le_iff_isOpen_imp` recovers the approximation order, forward by upward closure and backward by testing the basic open of each neighbourhood of the smaller element; `specializes_iff_le` is the same comparison read as Mathlib specialization in the reverse direction. The printed condition (ii) writes \([X]\in\mathcal{U}\), and every Lean statement proves \([X]\subseteq\mathcal{U}\). The Hausdorff question and both limit-point questions are not proved. The opening display \(\bigcap_{i<n}[X_i]\neq\emptyset\) is the finite-consistency criterion of Exercise 1.21. **Theorem:** partial — the topology and conditions (i)–(iii) are proved, with membership of \([X]\) repaired to containment, while separation and limit points remain open. **Proof:** partial — the basis axioms, the deduction of upward closure, and both directions of the order-recovery argument match those claims; there is no argument for Hausdorff separation or for limit points.
